# Comprehensive Spring Security Configuration Notes - All Types


---

## 1. PERMIT ALL Configuration

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) {
    http.authorizeHttpRequests(requests ->
            requests.anyRequest().permitAll());

    http.formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults());
    http.httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());
    return http.build();
}
```

### What It Does
- **Allows unrestricted access** to all endpoints
- **No authentication required** for any URL
- Form login and HTTP Basic auth are still configured but **optional**
- Users can access any resource without credentials

### Security Level
🔓 **OPEN** - Least secure (only for public/dev environments)

### Use Cases
- Public websites or landing pages
- Development/testing environments
- APIs with no security requirements
- Demo applications

### Example Usage

#### Request 1: Access Any Endpoint Without Login
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Account details (no login required)
```

#### Request 2: Access Protected Resources Directly
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/myBalance
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Balance information
```

#### Request 3: Try to Access /contact
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/contact
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Contact page
```

#### Request 4: Access Non-existent Endpoint
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/admin/users
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK (if endpoint exists)
or 404 (if not found - but not due to security)
```

### Configuration Analysis

| Setting | Value | Meaning |
|---------|-------|---------|
| `anyRequest()` | `permitAll()` | All requests allowed |
| `formLogin()` | Enabled | Login form available (but not required) |
| `httpBasic()` | Enabled | Basic auth available (but not required) |
| CSRF | Default enabled | Can be bypassed |

### Real-World Scenario
```
Scenario: Public Blog Website
- Homepage: ✅ Accessible
- Article page: ✅ Accessible
- Comments: ✅ Accessible
- Admin panel: ✅ Accessible (PROBLEM! Security issue)
```

### When NOT to Use
❌ Production environments
❌ Applications with sensitive data
❌ Systems requiring user authentication

---

## 2. DENY ALL Configuration

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) {
    http.authorizeHttpRequests(requests ->
            requests.anyRequest().denyAll());

    http.formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults());
    http.httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());
    return http.build();
}
```

### What It Does
- **Blocks access to ALL endpoints** without exception
- **Even authenticated users cannot access** resources
- Form login and HTTP Basic auth are configured but **won't help**
- Creates a completely locked-down application

### Security Level
🔒 **COMPLETELY LOCKED** - Most restrictive

### Use Cases
- Maintenance mode activation
- Emergency shutdown/lockdown
- Application under security audit
- Testing authorization denial
- Temporary service suspension

### Example Usage

#### Request 1: Try to Access Public Endpoint
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/contact
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 403 Forbidden
Body: Access Denied
```

#### Request 2: Try with Valid Credentials (Form Login)
```bash
curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/login \
  -d "username=user&password=password"
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 403 Forbidden
Body: Access Denied (even though login succeeded)
```

#### Request 3: Try HTTP Basic Authentication
```bash
curl -u validuser:validpass http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 403 Forbidden
Body: Access Denied (credentials ignored)
```

#### Request 4: Try to Access Error Page
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/error
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 403 Forbidden
Body: Access Denied
```

### Configuration Analysis

| Setting | Value | Meaning |
|---------|-------|---------|
| `anyRequest()` | `denyAll()` | All requests blocked |
| `formLogin()` | Enabled but useless | Won't grant access |
| `httpBasic()` | Enabled but useless | Won't grant access |
| Authentication | Irrelevant | Cannot bypass denial |

### Real-World Scenario
```
Scenario: Bank Under Maintenance
- Normal time: Application working normally
- Maintenance window (2 AM - 4 AM):
  - All endpoints blocked
  - Even admin cannot access
  - Users see: "Service Temporarily Unavailable"
  - Everyone gets 403 Forbidden
```

### Activation Example
```java
// Switch to DENY ALL for maintenance
@Bean
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "app.maintenance.enabled", havingValue = "true")
SecurityFilterChain maintenanceMode(HttpSecurity http) {
    http.authorizeHttpRequests(requests -> requests.anyRequest().denyAll());
    return http.build();
}
```

### When to Use
✅ Emergency lockdown
✅ Maintenance windows
✅ Security incidents
✅ Testing denial scenarios

---

## 3. SELECTIVE AUTHENTICATION (Currently Active)

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) {
    http.authorizeHttpRequests(requests -> requests
                    .requestMatchers("/myAccount", "/myBalance","/myLoans","/myCards").authenticated()
                    .requestMatchers("/contact","/notices","/error").permitAll()
            );

    http.formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults());
    http.httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());
    return http.build();
}
```

### What It Does
- **Divides endpoints into two categories:**
  1. **Protected URLs** - Require authentication
  2. **Public URLs** - No authentication needed
- **Two authentication methods** available: Form Login + HTTP Basic
- **Most common pattern** in real applications

### Security Level
🟢 **BALANCED** - Good security with usability

### Endpoint Categories

#### Protected Endpoints (Require Login)
- `/myAccount` - User account details
- `/myBalance` - Account balance
- `/myLoans` - Loan information
- `/myCards` - Credit card details

#### Public Endpoints (Open Access)
- `/contact` - Contact information
- `/notices` - Public notices/announcements
- `/error` - Error handling page

### Example Usage

#### Scenario 1: Anonymous User Accessing Public Content
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/contact
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Contact page content
```

#### Scenario 2: Anonymous User Accessing Protected Content
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 302 Found
Location: http://localhost:8080/login
Headers: Redirect to login form
```
**Browser receives login page with form**

#### Scenario 3: User Logs in via Form
```bash
# Step 1: GET login page
curl http://localhost:8080/login

# Step 2: Submit credentials
curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/login \
  -d "username=john&password=secret123" \
  -c cookies.txt

# Step 3: Access protected resource with session
curl -b cookies.txt http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: User account details
```

#### Scenario 4: Using HTTP Basic Authentication
```bash
curl -u john:secret123 http://localhost:8080/myBalance
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Account balance information
```

#### Scenario 5: Invalid Credentials
```bash
curl -u john:wrongpassword http://localhost:8080/myLoans
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 401 Unauthorized
WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm="Realm"
Body: Unauthorized
```

#### Scenario 6: Access Error Page Without Auth
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/error
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Error page (publicly accessible)
```

### Complete Access Matrix

| Endpoint | Anonymous | Form Login | HTTP Basic | Role-Based |
|----------|-----------|-----------|-----------|-----------|
| `/contact` | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| `/notices` | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| `/error` | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| `/myAccount` | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| `/myBalance` | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| `/myLoans` | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| `/myCards` | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |

### Real-World Scenario
```
Bank Application Flow:

1. User visits website
   → Landing page (/contact): ✅ Accessible without login

2. User clicks "Check Balance"
   → Browser redirected to login page
   → Login form displayed

3. User enters credentials
   → POST /login with username & password
   → Session created (session cookie stored)

4. User accesses /myBalance
   → Sends session cookie
   → Authorization check passes
   → Account balance displayed

5. User clicks "Contact Us"
   → /contact page loaded
   → No authentication check needed
```

### Configuration Details

```java
// Method 1: Direct path matching
.requestMatchers("/myAccount", "/myBalance").authenticated()

// Method 2: Pattern matching
.requestMatchers("/user/**").authenticated()
.requestMatchers("/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")

// Method 3: Regex patterns
.requestMatchers(RegexRequestMatcher).authenticated()

// Method 4: Custom matcher
.requestMatchers(request ->
    request.getMethod().equals("DELETE")
).authenticated()
```

### Security Flow Diagram
```
User Request
    ↓
Is endpoint in public list?
    ├─ YES → Proceed without authentication
    └─ NO → Check if user authenticated?
           ├─ YES → Proceed with access
           └─ NO → Redirect to login
```

### When to Use
✅ Standard web applications
✅ E-commerce sites
✅ Banking systems
✅ SaaS applications
✅ Most production systems

---

## 4. DISABLED FORM LOGIN Configuration

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) {
    http.authorizeHttpRequests(requests -> requests
            .requestMatchers("/myAccount", "/myBalance","/myLoans","/myCards").authenticated()
            .requestMatchers("/contact","/notices","/error").permitAll()
    );

    http.formLogin(flc -> flc.disable());
    http.httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());
    return http.build();
}
```

### What It Does
- **Authorization rules same as before**
- **Form login DISABLED** - No HTML login form available
- **HTTP Basic Auth ENABLED** - Only method to authenticate
- Users cannot use traditional username/password form

### Security Level
🟡 **SECURE FOR APIs** - Not suitable for browser-based apps

### Use Cases
- REST APIs requiring authentication
- Mobile applications
- Microservices communication
- Token-based authentication systems
- Programmatic access only

### Example Usage

#### Scenario 1: Try to Access Login Form (FAILS)
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/login
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 404 Not Found
Body: Login form not available
```

#### Scenario 2: Try to Access Protected Resource (REDIRECTS NOWHERE)
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 401 Unauthorized
Body: Unauthorized (no login form to redirect to)
```

#### Scenario 3: Use HTTP Basic Authentication (SUCCESS)
```bash
curl -u john:secret123 http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Account details
```

#### Scenario 4: Access Public Endpoint
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/contact
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Contact page
```

#### Scenario 5: API Call with Bearer Token (If JWT Added)
```bash
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGc..." http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Account details (if JWT valid)
```

#### Scenario 6: Missing Credentials in API Call
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/myBalance
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 401 Unauthorized
WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm="Realm"
```

### Real-World Scenario: REST API Server
```
Mobile App → API Server Configuration

1. Mobile app makes request
   GET /api/user/balance
   Headers: Authorization: Basic dXNlcjpwYXNz

2. Server processes request
   - Form login: DISABLED (not needed for app)
   - HTTP Basic: ENABLED (app sends credentials)
   - Extract credentials from header

3. Server responds
   Status: 200 OK
   Body: {"balance": 5000}
```

### Common Use: Microservices
```java
// Service A calling Service B
RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();
HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
headers.setBasicAuth("service-user", "service-pass");

HttpEntity<String> entity = new HttpEntity<>(headers);
ResponseEntity<String> response = restTemplate.exchange(
    "http://service-b:8080/data",
    HttpMethod.GET,
    entity,
    String.class
);
```

### Authentication Methods Comparison

| Method | Form Login | HTTP Basic | JWT Token |
|--------|-----------|-----------|-----------|
| Available | ❌ Disabled | ✅ Enabled | ❌ Not configured |
| Browser-friendly | N/A | ⚠️ Not recommended | ✅ With cookie |
| API-friendly | ❌ Not ideal | ✅ Simple | ✅ Better |
| Security | N/A | ⚠️ Basic encoding | ✅ Signed tokens |

### When to Use
✅ REST APIs
✅ Microservices
✅ Mobile backend
✅ Stateless services
✅ Token-based systems

### When NOT to Use
❌ Web applications with browsers
❌ Traditional form-based login needed
❌ User-facing UI applications

---

## 5. DISABLED HTTP BASIC Configuration

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) {
    http.authorizeHttpRequests(requests -> requests
            .requestMatchers("/myAccount", "/myBalance","/myLoans","/myCards").authenticated()
            .requestMatchers("/contact","/notices","/error").permitAll()
    );

    http.formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults());
    http.httpBasic(hbc -> hbc.disable());
    return http.build();
}
```

### What It Does
- **Authorization rules same as before**
- **HTTP Basic Auth DISABLED** - Cannot use Basic authentication
- **Form login ENABLED** - Only method available
- Requires traditional username/password form interaction

### Security Level
🟢 **GOOD FOR WEB APPS** - Browser-based login only

### Use Cases
- Web applications with UI
- Traditional web frameworks
- Server-side rendered applications
- Applications requiring session management
- User-facing applications

### Example Usage

#### Scenario 1: Try HTTP Basic Authentication (FAILS)
```bash
curl -u john:secret123 http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 302 Found
Location: http://localhost:8080/login
Headers: Credentials in basic format ignored
```

#### Scenario 2: Access Protected Resource Prompts Login Form
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 302 Found
Location: http://localhost:8080/login
Body: Redirects to login form
```

#### Scenario 3: Browser Access (Automatic Redirect)
```bash
# User types in browser: http://localhost:8080/myAccount
→ Browser automatically redirected to /login
→ Login form displayed with Username and Password fields
```

#### Scenario 4: Submit Form Credentials
```bash
curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/login \
  -d "username=john&password=secret123" \
  -d "_csrf=token123" \
  -c cookies.txt
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 302 Found
Location: http://localhost:8080/myAccount
Set-Cookie: JSESSIONID=ABC123
```

#### Scenario 5: Access with Session Cookie
```bash
curl -b cookies.txt http://localhost:8080/myBalance
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Balance information
```

#### Scenario 6: Public Endpoint Still Works
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/contact
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Contact page
```

### Real-World Scenario: Traditional Web App
```
Banking Website User Flow:

1. User visits website
   Browser: http://localhost:8080/
   Status: 200 OK (landing page loaded)

2. User clicks "Login"
   Browser: http://localhost:8080/myAccount
   Status: 302 Redirect
   Redirect: http://localhost:8080/login

3. Login form displayed
   Form fields:
   - Username: ___________
   - Password: ___________
   - [Login Button]

4. User enters credentials and clicks Login
   POST http://localhost:8080/login
   - username=john@bank.com
   - password=SecurePass123
   - _csrf=token

5. Server validates and creates session
   Response: 302 Redirect to /myAccount
   Cookie: JSESSIONID=ABCD1234567890

6. Browser follows redirect with session cookie
   GET http://localhost:8080/myAccount
   Cookie: JSESSIONID=ABCD1234567890
   Status: 200 OK
   Body: Account dashboard displayed
```

### Session Management Details
```
Session Storage:
- Server-side: Session data stored in server memory
- Client-side: Session ID sent in cookie
- Cookie name: JSESSIONID (default)
- Duration: Configurable (default 30 minutes)

Security Features:
- Session ID tied to specific browser/IP
- Cookie marked as HttpOnly (not accessible via JS)
- CSRF token generated for form submissions
- Automatic cleanup of expired sessions
```

### Configuration for Session Timeout
```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
        .authorizeHttpRequests(requests -> requests
            .requestMatchers("/myAccount").authenticated()
            .requestMatchers("/contact").permitAll()
        )
        .sessionManagement(session -> session
            .sessionFixationProtection(SessionFixationProtectionStrategy.MIGRATE_SESSION)
            .sessionConcurrency(concurrency -> concurrency
                .maximumSessions(1)  // One session per user
                .expiredUrl("/login?expired")
            )
        )
        .formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults());

    return http.build();
}
```

### When to Use
✅ Web applications
✅ Traditional server-rendered apps
✅ Applications requiring sessions
✅ Form-based login pages
✅ Most web frameworks (JSP, Thymeleaf, etc.)

### When NOT to Use
❌ REST APIs
❌ Mobile applications
❌ Stateless services
❌ Microservices

---

## 6. DISABLED BOTH (Form Login & HTTP Basic) Configuration

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) {
    http.authorizeHttpRequests(requests -> requests
            .requestMatchers("/myAccount", "/myBalance","/myLoans","/myCards").authenticated()
            .requestMatchers("/contact","/notices","/error").permitAll()
    );

    http.formLogin(flc -> flc.disable());
    http.httpBasic(hbc -> hbc.disable());
    return http.build();
}
```

### What It Does
- **Authorization rules same as before** (selective authentication)
- **Form login DISABLED** - No HTML login form
- **HTTP Basic auth DISABLED** - Cannot use basic credentials
- **No default authentication method** - Must use custom authentication
- Requires alternative authentication mechanism

### Security Level
🔵 **CUSTOM SECURITY** - Depends on additional configuration

### Use Cases
- OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect authentication
- JWT (JSON Web Token) based systems
- Custom authentication filters
- API Gateway authentication
- Single Sign-On (SSO) systems
- Certificate-based authentication
- Multi-factor authentication (MFA)

### Example Usage

#### Scenario 1: Try Form Login (FAILS)
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/login
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 404 Not Found
Body: Login endpoint not available
```

#### Scenario 2: Try HTTP Basic Authentication (FAILS)
```bash
curl -u john:secret123 http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 401 Unauthorized
Body: Unauthorized (basic auth disabled)
```

#### Scenario 3: Try to Access Protected Resource
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/myBalance
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 403 Forbidden
Body: Access Denied (no authentication method available)
```

#### Scenario 4: Public Endpoint Still Accessible
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/contact
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Contact page
```

### With JWT Token Authentication Added

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
        .authorizeHttpRequests(requests -> requests
            .requestMatchers("/myAccount", "/myBalance").authenticated()
            .requestMatchers("/contact", "/notices", "/error").permitAll()
        )
        .formLogin(flc -> flc.disable())
        .httpBasic(hbc -> hbc.disable())
        .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 ->
            oauth2.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults())
        );

    return http.build();
}
```

#### Scenario 5: Access with JWT Token (SUCCESS)
```bash
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..." \
     http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 200 OK
Body: Account details
```

#### Scenario 6: Without Token (FAILS)
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/myAccount
```
**Response:**
```
Status: 401 Unauthorized
WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm="Realm"
Body: Unauthorized
```

### Real-World Scenario: OAuth 2.0 Protected API

```java
@Configuration
public class SecurityConfiguration {

    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(requests -> requests
                .requestMatchers("/public/**").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .formLogin(flc -> flc.disable())
            .httpBasic(hbc -> hbc.disable())
            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 ->
                oauth2.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults())
            );

        return http.build();
    }
}
```

**Usage Flow:**
```
1. Client requests access token from OAuth provider
   POST https://oauth-provider.com/token
   {
       "client_id": "my-app",
       "client_secret": "secret",
       "grant_type": "password",
       "username": "user",
       "password": "pass"
   }

2. OAuth provider returns JWT token
   Response:
   {
       "access_token": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIs...",
       "token_type": "Bearer",
       "expires_in": 3600
   }

3. Client includes token in API requests
   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIs..." \
        http://localhost:8080/myAccount

4. Server validates token and grants access
   Status: 200 OK
   Body: Protected resource
```

### Example with Certificate-Based Authentication

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
        .authorizeHttpRequests(requests -> requests
            .requestMatchers("/myAccount").authenticated()
            .requestMatchers("/contact").permitAll()
        )
        .formLogin(flc -> flc.disable())
        .httpBasic(hbc -> hbc.disable())
        .x509(Customizer.withDefaults());  // Client certificate auth

    return http.build();
}
```

**Usage:**
```bash
# Client presents certificate
curl --cert client-cert.pem \
     --key client-key.pem \
     https://localhost:8080/myAccount
```

### Example with SAML Authentication

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
        .authorizeHttpRequests(requests -> requests
            .requestMatchers("/myAccount").authenticated()
            .requestMatchers("/contact").permitAll()
        )
        .formLogin(flc -> flc.disable())
        .httpBasic(hbc -> hbc.disable())
        .saml2Login(Customizer.withDefaults());  // SAML 2.0 auth

    return http.build();
}
```

### Real-World Scenario: Enterprise App with Azure AD

```
User → Application → Azure AD Login
   ↓
   Authentication Code
   ↓
   Exchange Code for Token
   ↓
   Access Protected Resources

Configuration:
- Form Login: ❌ Disabled
- HTTP Basic: ❌ Disabled
- SAML 2.0: ✅ Enabled (Azure AD provider)
- Session: Created after successful SAML auth
```

### Authentication Method Comparison Matrix

| Method | Form | Basic | JWT | OAuth | SAML | Cert |
|--------|------|-------|-----|-------|------|------|
| Web App | ✅ | ⚠️ | ⚠️ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ |
| REST API | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ |
| Mobile | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ |
| Enterprise | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Microservices | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ |

### When to Use
✅ OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect
✅ JWT token-based APIs
✅ Enterprise SSO systems
✅ Certificate-based systems
✅ Custom authentication logic
✅ Multi-provider authentication

### When NOT to Use
❌ Simple web applications
❌ When form login is sufficient
❌ Applications without alternative auth providers

---

## Summary Table: All 6 Configurations

| Configuration | Form Login | HTTP Basic | Public URLs | Protected URLs | Best For |
|---------------|-----------|-----------|------------|----------------|----------|
| 1. Permit All | Enabled | Enabled | All | None | Dev/Testing |
| 2. Deny All | Enabled | Enabled | None | All | Maintenance |
| 3. Selective Auth | Enabled | Enabled | Some | Some | **Production** |
| 4. No Form Login | Disabled | Enabled | Some | Some | REST APIs |
| 5. No HTTP Basic | Enabled | Disabled | Some | Some | Web Apps |
| 6. No Both | Disabled | Disabled | Some | Some | OAuth/JWT/SSO |

---

## Quick Reference: Which Configuration to Use?

```
Decision Tree:

Is this a public application?
├─ YES, completely open
│  └─ Use: PERMIT ALL (Config 1)
│
├─ NO, temporary lockdown needed
│  └─ Use: DENY ALL (Config 2)
│
└─ MAYBE, some parts public, some private
   ├─ Is this a web application with forms?
   │  └─ Use: SELECTIVE AUTH (Config 3) or NO HTTP BASIC (Config 5)
   │
   ├─ Is this a REST API?
   │  └─ Use: SELECTIVE AUTH (Config 3) or NO FORM LOGIN (Config 4)
   │
   └─ Do you need OAuth/JWT/SSO?
      └─ Use: DISABLED BOTH (Config 6)
```

This covers all six security configurations in your Spring Security file with comprehensive examples and real-world scenarios!